sfied","summary":"Pre-reboot boot_id cf34dc87... captured; reboot issued with observed connection loss; post-reboot boot_id f499ebdc... confirmed changed; rabbitmq-server active, cluster_status shows 3/3 running, iptables 15672 rules intact. Consistent with fixed lifecycle boot-identity cross-check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-87d5f9e65eef4eda","cmd-24245756e0934898","cmd-1cba2d4bde1548ed","cmd-f9596a20767249d8"]},{"id":"req-7","requirement":"node3 restart: healthy baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered cluster/data/firewall/TLS state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Pre-reboot boot_id 63f96994... captured; reboot issued with observed connection loss; post-reboot boot_id 1af46fc6... confirmed changed; rabbitmq-server active, cluster_status shows 3/3 running, iptables 15672 rules intact. Consistent with fixed lifecycle boot-identity cross-check. End-to-end AMQPS messaging and TLS client-cert enforcement re-verified against all nodes after the full restart cycle.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d7431a8f064a4e5b","cmd-84d8085c5924445d","cmd-47bb1c5c107f46cf","cmd-47ddf9670e6446a8","cmd-a485c09daecd4652"]}],"confidence":0.85,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Most state-changing commands map cleanly to task outcomes: package installs, CA/cert generation, TLS config, cluster join, iptables rules (persisted), user management, and the restart protocol. The temporary HTTP file server used to distribute certs to node2/node3 (cmd-37cd1e1adaf04ab9) was bounded and explicitly torn down afterward (cmd-1c9dc408e96d4d19), which is good practice. One unnecessary mutation was identified: cmd-ff791179a4964a92 wrote an SSH private key to ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 and appended a public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on node1 in an attempt to set up SSH-based file distribution to node2/node3; this attempt failed (return_code 255, sshd inactive on peers) and the executor pivoted to the HTTP-server approach instead. No subsequent command was captured removing the written SSH key material, so this residue (low sensitivity, but an added credential/authorized_keys entry) was not shown to be cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ff791179a4964a92","cmd-37cd1e1adaf04ab9","cmd-1c9dc408e96d4d19"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a 3-node RabbitMQ cluster with AMQP served exclusively over TLS on 5671, backed by a dedicated cluster CA, and enforced mutual TLS (verify_peer + fail_if_no_peer_cert) such that only CA-issued client certificates are accepted — directly demonstrated via positive (valid cert) and negative (no cert, foreign-CA cert) TLS handshake tests, plus end-to-end AMQPS pub/sub. The management interface is bound to each node's private IP and firewalled via iptables/netfilter-persistent to the three cluster IPs with a catch-all DROP; reachability from within the cluster was verified, though no test captured a blocked connection from outside the allowlist. The mandatory one-at-a-time restart protocol was executed for all three nodes with clear pre/post boot-identity changes, connection-loss evidence during the reboot, and confirmed recovery of cluster membership, firewall rules, and TLS/AMQP functionality afterward, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. One unnecessary, unreverted mutation (an abandoned SSH key/authorized_keys setup) was found during hygiene review.","limitations":["No captured evidence of an actual blocked connection attempt to the management API from an IP outside the three-node cluster allowlist; the exclusion relies on iptables rule inspection and interface binding rather than an observed negative test.","No cleanup command was captured for the SSH key material written to node1's ~/.ssh directory during the abandoned SSH-based file-distribution attempt, so its final state is unconfirmed.","Restart-window observations rely on repeated connection-reset/timeout polling rather than a continuous log capture, so the exact disruption boundary is inferred rather than directly logged."]},"ttft